<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yamunotri</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yamunotri is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrimage site in the Uttarkashi district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, near the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> river in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a>. Its temple to the goddess Yamuna and nearby hot springs make it the first stop of the Char Dham pilgrimage, which continues to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangotri">Gangotri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kedarnath">Kedarnath</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badrinath">Badrinath</a>. Pilgrims reach it on foot from the road head at Janki Chatti.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yamunotri.jpg" alt="Yamunotri" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Atarax42 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yamuna_at_Yamunotri.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
